  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Weathers shut down St. Louis over six scoreless innings and the offense scratched out just enough to hold.",
    "what_this_means": "They led from the second inning forward, never trailing once, but they couldn't put the game away when it mattered. In the fourth, they had runners on with runners in scoring position and managed nothing. Left two on base in a critical spot when the margin was still negotiable. That's the difference between 2\u20130 and 5\u20130: execution when the window is open. Bednar locked down the ninth with three strikeouts, and the lead held. But the offense generated only five hits and five strikeouts. This is how fragile wins look. Pitching good enough to survive, bats doing just enough to matter, and nothing resembling dominant play from either side. The margin sits at one game back in the division, and it shrinks against teams that make more out of similar chances. They won the pitching game. They didn't win the hitting game.",
    "what_to_watch": "St. Louis has Andre Pallante on the mound tonight, a 3.72 ERA starter the Yankees saw clean through last time. Warren takes the ball with a chance to build real momentum instead of another narrow edge.",
